WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.270
He's one of the greatest stand-up comedians
of all-time, and when he gave that up, he

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.360
became a massive movie star, not to mention
one of the most popular Saturday Night Live

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.360
hosts ever.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.969
Yet in recent years, Steve Martin has all
but disappeared from both the big screen and

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:14.969
the small.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:16.199
So what's he been up to instead?

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:20.089
Here's a look at the real reason we don't
hear from Steve Martin anymore.

00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:21.810
Busy with bluegrass

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:26.230
Banjo-playing once played a major role in
Martin's stand-up routines, but his banjo

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.050
skills are actually no joke.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.970
In the late 2000s, Martin reinvented himself
as a serious musician.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.020
How serious?

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:37.820
Not only has he had a number one hit single
and performed at the Grand Ole Opry several

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:42.270
times, he even won a Grammy for the song "Love
Has Come for You" with musical partner Edie

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.330
Brickell.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:44.390
"Love.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.440
Love.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.740
Love has come for you."

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:54.960
Martin thinks there's something mysterious
and uniquely American about the banjo, telling

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.500
NPR "There's something very compelling about
it to certain people.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.520
I'd like to think it's because we're Americans
and the banjo is truly an American instrument,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.640
and it captures something about our past."

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.190
The write stuff

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:12.330
Considering Martin wrote the scripts for several
hit films, including Roxanne, Bowfinger, and

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.500
L.A. Story, it's not really a surprise that
he's been just as successful in the publishing

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.909
business as he has been in the movie business.

00:01:18.909 --> 00:01:24.730
In 2000, his novella Shopgirl was a top ten
New York Times Bestseller for 15 weeks, and

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:28.880
he later followed that up with the novels
The Pleasure of My Company and An Object of

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:29.880
Beauty.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.300
His most successful book, though, isn't fiction,
but rather his 2007 memoir Born Standing Up:

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.730
A Comic's Life, which chronicles his rise
to the top of the standup world before abruptly

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:42.110
quitting forever in 1981.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:47.130
Martin told NPR he sees the book as less about
comedy and more about the journey of a self-made

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.130
man.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.799
"I think it's somehow an American story, because
I started untalented.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:54.240
I didn't have any gifts except perseverance."

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.880
The Great White Way

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.280
Given Steve Martin's fondness for both music
and writing, it makes sense that the star

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.600
would eventually set his sights on Broadway.

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:08.200
In 2014, Martin and Brickell wrote and produced
Bright Star, a musical drama inspired by the

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.259
duo's album Love Has Come for You.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.560
The play made its way to Broadway in 2016,
where it racked up five Tony nominations,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.939
including Best Musical, Best Book in a Musical,
and Best Score, but lost to the cultural juggernaut

00:02:20.939 --> 00:02:21.939
known as Hamilton.

00:02:21.939 --> 00:02:25.529
But Bright Star wasn't Martin's first foray
into playwriting.

00:02:25.529 --> 00:02:31.629
In 1993, Martin penned Picasso at the Lapin
Agile, which depicts a conversation between

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:33.659
Pablo Picasso and Albert Einstein.

00:02:33.659 --> 00:02:39.060
The play made international headlines in 2009,
when outraged parents objected to the characters'

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:43.870
"questionable behavior" and successfully prevented
students at Oregon's La Grande High School

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.669
from mounting a production.

00:02:45.669 --> 00:02:50.129
Martin responded by offering to fund an off-campus
production of Picasso and penned a scathing

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:54.840
editorial in the La Grand Observer in which
he argued that, despite the play's adult content,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.590
"the spirit of the play and its endorsement
of the arts and sciences are appropriate for

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.540
young eyes and minds."

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:02.540
Family man

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.830
Steve Martin tends to keep his private life
to himself.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:10.540
In 2007, he invited his closest friends over
for a dinner party which turned out to be

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:11.540
a surprise wedding.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.780
In 2012, when the actor was 67 years old,
Martin's wife, former New York Times staffer

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:22.120
Anne Stringfield, gave birth to the comedian's
first child — and nobody knew about it for

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:23.120
months.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.639
In fact, the public still doesn't even know
what her name is, something Martin has made

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.120
light of in interviews.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.030
"Thought about the name quite a bit.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.969
'Conquistador,' what do you think?"

00:03:31.969 --> 00:03:34.480
Being a dad has changed Martin's priorities.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.430
He told The Telegraph, "When I was younger,
I was selfish and focused on my career.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.659
Now, I'm just hanging around the house playing
with [my daughter].

00:03:41.659 --> 00:03:42.659
It's great."

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:44.650
Hi, art

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.770
Ever since he bought his first print in 1968,
Martin has been an avid art collector, with

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:54.019
a collection that includes everything from
multiple Picassos to the grungy alternative

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:56.980
cartoons of American comic book artist Robert
Crumb.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:01.860
"And the first thing I bought happened to
be an Ed Ruscha print of Hollywood.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.870
The Hollywood sign."

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:07.939
While Martin usually keeps the details of
his personal collection characteristically

00:04:07.939 --> 00:04:12.650
private, the comedian used his fame to bring
attention to a little-known Canadian artist

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:17.930
named Lawren Harris by organizing an exhibition
that appeared in Los Angeles, Boston, and

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:18.930
Toronto.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.250
Of course, for Martin, art collecting isn't
all fun and games.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:29.919
In 2004, he bought "Landscape With Horses"
by Dutch painter Heinrich Campendonk for $850,000,

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:34.789
only to learn later that the piece was one
of 44 forgeries produced by a group of German

00:04:34.789 --> 00:04:35.789
criminals.

00:04:35.789 --> 00:04:38.949
Even then, Martin was able to take it all
in stride.

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:40.270
Sit-down comedy

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:45.460
In February, 2016, Steve Martin took the stage
at New York's Beacon Theater and delivered

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.849
a 10-minute stand-up set — his first in
35 years.

00:04:48.849 --> 00:04:53.770
It was a favor to headliner Jerry Seinfeld
after an appearance on Seinfeld's Comedians

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:58.340
in Cars Getting Coffee, where Martin explained
why he suddenly quit stand-up at the top of

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.610
his game in 1981.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:04.810
"Part of my goal was to be absurd in a very
serious time.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:08.120
I could tell that the era was ending too.

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.599
I could feel it."

00:05:09.599 --> 00:05:11.349
Old man Martin

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:16.110
Let's face it: even for a young man, filming
a movie can be a grueling experience.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:20.430
Despite Hollywood's glamorous image, actors
often work extremely long hours, get very

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:25.200
little sleep, and don't always get days off,
often spending weeks away from home.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.690
Martin told Biography, now that he's in his
seventies and has a family, that stuff doesn't

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:30.970
sound so appealing anymore.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.270
"It's difficult, the process of making a movie.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:37.690
If a studio had its way, they'd have you working
14 hours a day, every day.

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.789
As you get older, your life actually becomes
more important than your career, believe it

00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:42.789
or not."

00:05:42.789 --> 00:05:44.259
He could make a comeback

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:48.240
Martin may be spending his time out of the
spotlight now, but he's still one of the most

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.320
respected names in Hollywood, both for his
comedic and dramatic acting skills.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:55.389
There's no question he could make a comeback
if he wanted to.

00:05:55.389 --> 00:05:59.900
But between his music, his plays, his novels,
and his family, right now it doesn't seem

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.150
like making movies is a priority for him.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.419
To which there's really only one one appropriate
response:

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:09.180
"Well, excuuuuuuse me!"

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:12.940
Thanks for watching!

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:15.669
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:18.000
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

